% holding-kind: fragment
% holding-kind-note: One bar, which four men repeated while they clapped and danced (the moves are in the N: line). It would want the clap pattern as a rhythm part, the dance as steps for the class, and how many times round it went.
% source-url: https://archive.org/details/musicofhindostan00foxs/page/57/mode/1up archive.org scan of The Music of Hindostan, at p. 57
X:1
T:Gurkha dance tune
T:Gurkha dance, four men clapping (one bar, repeated)
C:traditional (Gurkha, Nepal)
O:India, Dharmsala (2nd Goorkhas)
R:dance
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 127, p. 57
N:"To such tunes as" this, four men A, B, C, D danced, lifting the foot to the level of the knee, their movements on the four beats of the bar, which the plate numbers 1 2 3 0 (0 is the silent beat): (1) all clap hands; (2) B and D touch right palm to A and C's left palm, fingers pointing upwards; (3) clap hands; (0) D and B touch left palm to A and C's right palm, and all step backwards instead of forwards on this beat. No words, no tempo and no tonic are printed; no signature, and the notes are d, c and G, so the drone is on G and the band's clap line marks the three hand-beats and leaves beat 0 silent.
N:HOW READ (2026-11-09): one staff, cropped at 1200, 1600 and 2400 dpi (scratch crops w-ex127-*, n-ex127-*, d-ex127-*); pitches from the detector (d G G, d d, c, G) with the second and third notes of beat 3 and the c of beat 0 read by eye; one pass only.
N:UNCERTAIN: the rhythm of beats 1 and 3 (written eighth and two sixteenths; a triplet is possible), the pitch of the second and third notes of beat 3, and the rest that closes beat 0.
